DA is generally for solid metal hydrides in which, after surface adsorption, molecular hydrogen is dissociated into atoms that form a solid solution of a hydride phase.1,3 Although metal hydrides show high hydrogen storage capacities at low pressure and high volumetric densities, they suffer from large barriers in dissociating the $\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bond to store the hydrogen and large barriers in recombining the H atoms to desorb $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Also, due to high binding energies for binding the hydrogen atoms to the metal hosts, one should manage the heat generated during the hydrogen charging/ discharging process to apply the DA method into on-board vehicles. In contrast, AA binds the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as a molecule by van der Waals interactions between the physisorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and the host material, and thus the binding energy is typically less than $10\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ .4 This indicates that there are no problems with reversibility and large heat release on charging of hydrogen. However this low interaction energy means that adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ takes place only at low temperatures such as $77~\mathrm{K}$ . But here the challenge has been to obtain sufficiently strong bonding to molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to achieve the 2010 US DOE (Department of Energy) target of $6.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $45\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1}}$ near room temperature ( $-30$ to $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) with pressures $\leq100$ bar.5
Until now, hydrogen storage using AA has been studied mainly with porous materials that show large surface areas, such as zeolites,6 carbon materials (activated carbon7 and nanotube structures8), BN nanotubes,9 and polymers.10 However the evolution of new porous materials called metal– organic frameworks (MOFs) provides a new vision into the AA method where the MOFs are crystalline materials composed of metal oxide and organic units.11,12
The Yaghi group13 first reported that a MOF (MOF-5) with a surface area of $3534~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , where inorganic $\mathrm{[OZn_{4}]^{6+}}$ groups are joined to an octahedral array of $[\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}-\mathrm{CO}_{2}]^{2-}$ (1,4-benzenedicarboxylate, BDC) groups to form a porous cubic framework, showed $4.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at $^{78\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 0.8 bar and it stored $1\mathrm{\textrm{wt\%}}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 20 bar. Although the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount for MOF-5 at $77~\mathrm{K}$ was reported again later as $1.30^{14}\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at 1.01 bar (1 atm) and $1.51^{15}\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 1.13 bar, the result was still exciting enough to activate many researchers to study this topic. Representatively, Wong-Foy et al.16 reported a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount of $7.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $32\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 70 bar for MOF-177 with a surface area of $4746~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the framework consisting of tetrahedral $\mathrm{[OZn_{4}]^{6+}}$ clusters linked by the tritopic linker BTB (1,3,5-benzenetribenzoate), and Dinca˜ et al.17 showed another MOF (surface area: $2100\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ with $5.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $43\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 45 bar, where the MOF has the formation of $\mathrm{\small{[Mn(DMF)_{6}]_{3}[(M n_{4}C l)_{3}(B T T)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{12}]_{2}}}$ $42\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}20\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ with a cubic topology, where $\begin{array}{r l}{{\bf D}{\bf M}{\bf F}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ dimethylformamide and $\mathbf{BTT}\ =\ 1{,}3{,}5{\mathrm{,}}$ -benzenetristetrazolate. As seen in these results, MOFs show higher reversible hydrogen uptake than any other porous materials.
Recently, the Yaghi group also synthesized twodimensional18,19 and three-dimensional20,21 covalent organic frameworks (COFs) where the organic building units are held together by strong covalent bonds, such as C–C, C–O, B–O, and $\mathrm{Si-C}$ , rather than metal ions to produce the materials. The COFs have high surface areas $(3472\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{\bfg}^{-1}$ for COF-102 and $4210~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for COF-103) similar to MOFs, as well as showing very low crystal densities $(0.17~\mathrm{~g~}\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$ for COF-108).20 These characteristics make COFs excellent candidates for the storage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
