In considering gas adsorption, one needs to distinguish excess adsorption and total (or absolute) adsorption.22–25 When an adsorbate gas (e.g. $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{H}_{2,\dag}^{\phantom{}\setminus}}\end{array}$ ) is in contact with a solid adsorbent (e.g. MOFs), the region near the solid surface is called the adsorbed phase, which arises from the gas–solid intermolecular forces of attraction. This adsorbed phase may extend several diameters of the adsorbate molecules from the solid surface. However, the size and structure of the adsorbed phase and the actual density and composition profiles of the adsorbates within the adsorbed phase are generally quite difficult to characterize experimentally. Consequently, their properties are unknown functions of the bulk gas phase pressure and system temperature. Therefore, the experimentally observed mass change in the sample is represented as the difference between the total adsorbed amount and the bulk density of the adsorbate, which is expressed by a simple equation:24,25
$$
N_{\mathrm{total}}=N_{\mathrm{excess}}+\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}V_{\mathrm{pore}}
$$
where $N_{\mathrm{total}}$ is the total adsorbed amount, $N_{\mathrm{excess}}$ is the excess amount, $\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}$ is the bulk density of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $V_{\mathrm{pore}}$ is the pore volume of an adsorbent. Thus, most experimental measurements are reported as an excess adsorption amount. However, the 2010 DOE target5 of $6.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $45\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1\1}}$ is in terms of the total amount, leading to the importance of this quantity.
In using eqn (1), $\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}$ is usually from the experimental NIST database26 and is experimentally measured in a free volume. (Here note that the $\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}$ is the bulk density.) However MOFs and COFs have pore sizes from 3.8 for IRMOF-5 to $28.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for
IRMOF-16.27 Thus, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ density in these confined volumes may be different from the $\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}$ k,28,29 making obtaining an accurate total amount by an experiment difficult. On the other hand, a computer simulation (e.g. grand canonical Monte-Carlo (GCMC)) could predict accurate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ density in the nanopores if one uses accurate force fields (FFs) between guest $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ –host (MOFs and COFs) and guest–guest, indicating that the theoretical method is very useful to obtain the total adsorption amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Moreover, in a computer simulation one is easily able to build structural models of MOFs and COFs, which are very helpful to find new materials with higher hydrogen capacities. Due to these advantages of computer simulations, many researchers have been using these virtual experiments to investigate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in MOFs and COFs.
Therefore, we will review here recent theoretical advances in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in MOFs and COFs, and then in detail discuss ways to improve the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage inferred from the theoretical and experimental studies reported so far.
## 2. Recent computational studies on hydrogen storage in MOFs and COFs
Recent computational studies are largely divided into three methodologies: ab initio or density-functional theory (DFT) calculations to investigate binding energies of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to MOFs and COFs, GCMC simulations to predict $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amounts in them, and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations to investigate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion in them.
2.1 Ab initio and DFT calculations on $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs and COFs
After the Yaghi group13 first reported the high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of MOF-5 in 2003, Hu¨ ber et al.30 most promptly investigated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to the MOF using a theoretical approach. They focused on the interaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with the aromatic systems $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}X}$ $\mathbf{\DeltaX}=\mathbf{\DeltaH}$ , F, OH, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , and CN), $\mathrm{{C_{10}H_{8}}}$ (naphthalene and azulene), $\mathrm{C_{14}H_{10}}$ (anthracene), $\mathrm{C}_{24}\mathrm{H}_{12}$ (coronene), $\scriptstyle p\mathbf{-C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathbf{COOH})_{2}$ (terephthalic acid), and $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{~}}&{{}}&{p{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathbf{COOLi})_{2}}\end{array}$ (dilithium terephthalate) using second-order Møller–Plesset $(\mathrm{MP}2)^{31}$ calculations with the approximate resolution of the identity $\left(\mathbf{R}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{P}2\right)^{32}$ in the TZVPP (triple- $\xi$ valence basis33 supplemented with the polarization functions of the cc-pVTZ basis34). The calculations show that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies to benzene and naphthalene are 3.91 and $4.28~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively, indicating that enlarging the aromatic system increases the interaction energy. In the same year (2004), similar works were reported by Sagara et al.35 and Hamel and Coˆ t ´e.36 Sagara et al.35 calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies to $\mathrm{H}_{2^{-1},4}$ -benzenedicarboxylate- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , the organic linker part in MOF-5, as well as to the $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O(H C O_{2})_{6}}}}$ cluster, the metal oxide part in MOF-5, using the MP2 methodology and showed that the zinc oxide cluster has a higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy than the organic linker part. And Hamel and $\mathrm{C}\hat{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{t}\acute{\mathrm{e}}^{36}$ calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy to benzene with ab initio methods such as MP2 and coupled cluster with noniterative triple excitation $[\mathrm{CCSD}(\mathrm{T})]^{37}$ as well as DFT methods of local-density approximation $\left(\mathrm{LDA}\right)^{38}$ and generalized gradient approximation $\left(\mathrm{GGA}\right)^{39}$ where they considered several $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ configurations on the benzene. Also they calculated theoretical rotational spectra of the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and found that the theoretical result is comparable to the experimental inelastic neutron scattering (INS) spectra for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed in MOFs.13,27 In 2005, Sagara et al.40,41 re-calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies for the organic linker part and $Z\mathrm{n}$ oxide part in MOF-5 (IRMOF-1) using MP2 with the quadruple zeta $\mathrm{Q}\mathrm{Z}\mathrm{VPP}^{33,34}$ basis set, larger than the previous TZVPP,35 in order to obtain more accurate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies. Also they calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions with organic linkers in various MOFs (IRMOF-1,27 IRMOF-3,27 IRMOF-6,27 IRMOF-8,27 IRMOF-12,27 IRMOF-14,27 IRMOF-18,14 and IRMOF-993) and found that the larger linkers showed the higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies and were able to bind multiple hydrogen molecules per side, and the addition of an $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group to each linker could provide up to a $33\%$ increase in the binding energy.40 After 2005, several MP2 or DFT works on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction with organic linkers of MOFs were reported by Buda and Dunietz42 (MP2 work), Lochan and HeadGordon43 (MP2), Negri and Saending44 (MP2 and DFT), Klontzas et al.45 (MP2 and DFT), Gao and $\mathrm{Zeng^{46}}$ (MP2 and DFT), Lee et al.47 (DFT), Han et al.48 (MP2), Sagara and Ganz49 (MP2), and Kuc et al.50 (MP2). Especially, for the first time we considered $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding with three different metal oxide clusters, namely $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O(H C O_{2})_{6}}}}$ , $\mathrm{Mg}_{4}\mathrm{O(HCO}_{2})_{6}$ and $\mathrm{\bfBe}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{6}$ , in IRMOF types using MP2/QZVPP calculations, and found that the substitution of metal sites from $Z\mathrm{n}$ to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ and Be does not change the basic configuration of the $\mathbf{M}_{4}\mathbf{O}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{6}$ cluster and the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ cluster has the strongest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy.48
With MP2 calculations, one is not able to consider periodic crystals at the current technical level. Instead, to calculate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies in MOF crystals, DFT calculations should be used. The DFT application to the MOF-5 crystal was tried by Mulder et al.,51 and Mueller and Ceder.52 Both papers showed that the strongest interactions with hydrogen are located near the $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}}}$ clusters although they reported different $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies to the Zn4O clusters (70 meV per H2 by Mulder et al.51 and $20\mathrm{meV}$ per $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ by Mueller and Ceder52), which is validated by some experimental53–57 and theoretical35,48,58 works. As shown so far, ab initio and DFT theories are very helpful to calculate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies to MOFs and then provide good information on the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption sites in MOFs.
Additionally, here we need to compare between the ab initio and DFT methods for the calculation of van der Waals interactions (e.g. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction with MOFs and COFs). Because of the nature of van der Waals interactions (induced dipole–dipole), the wave function must at least include double excitations from a Slater determinant reference corresponding to single excitations of each subsystem. This means that ab initio methods used should be at least of the level of MP2 and CCSD. However, since DFT methods do not consider the excitation, they do not provide accurate long-range dispersion interactions.59 In addition, it turns out that the triple excitations also have a very important contribution to the dispersion energy,60 and then the CCSD(T) method would be more accurate in calculating van der Waals interactions than MP2, however MP2 has a greater advantage in aspect of calculation time.
2.2 GCMC simulations for prediction of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity in MOFs and COFs
GCMC simulation is a commonly used technique to study gas adsorption properties in confined and bulk fluids.61 The simulation is conducted in a constant volume system defined by a simulation box, in which the number of fluid particles and configuration energy are allowed to fluctuate at constant temperature and chemical potential. Random microstates are generated based on the well-established Metropolis scheme,62 involving four types of movements: creation, deletion, displacement and rotation of fluid particles. Also there is a case ignoring the rotation of fluid particles if the particle means a gas molecule itself, not an atom in the gas molecule. Since a hydrogen molecule $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has a linear conformation, the ignoring of rotation during the GCMC algorithm may lead to inaccuracy in the simulation (on the other hand, ignoring rotation in the case of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake would be fine because $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ has a spherical conformation). Also during GCMC simulation, it is assumed that structures of adsorbent materials (e.g. MOFs and COFs) are fixed.
A study on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOF-5 by GCMC simulation was first tried by Sagara et al.35 in 2004 where they used the typical universal force field (UFF).63 The GCMC simulation showed that a simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ loading curve up to 1 bar at $^{78\mathrm{~K~}}$ was overestimated by $25\%$ in comparison to an experimental result.14 Yang and $Z{\mathrm{hong}}^{64}$ simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for IRMOF-1, IRMOF-8, and IRMOF-18 using GCMC and the OPLS force field (OPLS-AA)65 where they showed better results for IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-18 than Sagara et al.35 Garberoglio et al.66 calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for various MOFs (MOF-2,67 MOF-3,68 IRMOF-1,27 IRMOF-6,27 IRMOF-8,27 and IRMOF- $14^{27}$ ) with the $\mathrm{UFF}^{63}$ and DREIDING.69 Their simulations overestimated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of IRMOF-1, while underestimated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of IRMOF-8 up to 1 bar at $77\mathrm{K}$ , although the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of IRMOF-1 showed better agreement with an experiment through quantum effect correction70 of the hydrogen molecules. However the simulation reported the important fact that at room temperature all MOFs considered were not able to meet the DOE target.
Similarly, Yang and Zhong71 simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotherms at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ up to 7 MPa (70 bar) for IRMOF-1 and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC (also known as HKUST-1)72 using the OPLS-AA $\mathrm{FF}^{63}$ with additional refinement in the work and showed their simulation was in good agreement with experiments.73 They also extended their simulation technique into $\mathrm{MOF}{-}505^{74}$ with open metal sites,75 and found that the open metal sites have a favorable impact on the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs, however the MOF still showed low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at room temperature.75
Frost et al.76 clarified the effects of surface area, free volume, and heat of adsorption on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs by GCMC simulation with the DREIDING $\mathrm{FF}^{69}$ although their simulation tended to underestimate up to 1 bar and overestimate from the pressure owing to use of the empirical FF. They revealed the existence of three adsorption regimes: at low pressure (0.1 bar), hydrogen uptake correlates with the heat of adsorption; at intermediate pressure $(30\ \mathrm{bar})$ , uptake correlates with the surface area; and at the highest pressure (100 bar), uptake correlates with the free volume.
Jung et al.77 studied the effect of catenation on hydrogen adsorption onto the interpenetrating MOFs using the GCMC and UFF,63 and showed that the small pores generated by catenation can play a role to confine the hydrogen molecules more densely, so that the capacity of interpenetrating MOFs could be higher than that of the non-interpenetrating MOFs. Recently a similar work on the effect of catenation on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs was also reported by Ryan et al.78 where they used the DREIDING $\mathrm{FF}^{69}$ during GCMC simulations. According to the simulation,78 catenation can be beneficial for improving hydrogen storage in MOFs at cryogenic temperatures and low pressures, however not necessarily at room temperature.
Recently, the GCMC simulation has been applied to new MOFs which were experimentally synthesized. Liu et al.79 synthesized a new MOF material, $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{ted})_{0.5}]$ (bdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzenedicarboxylate, ted $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ triethylenediamine), and showed good agreement between experimental and simulational $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake isotherms at 77 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ where they used the $\mathrm{UFF}^{63}$ and DREIDING69 together with the quantum effect correction.70 However, they (Liu et al.)80 also applied the same simulational technique to the $\mathrm{Cu-BTC}^{72}$ MOF (the activation process of the sample was experimentally improved in the work) and showed a slight disagreement between simulations and experiments for the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ . And Noguchi et al.81 synthesized a new $\mathrm{cu}$ -based MOF $\mathrm{[Cu(4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) ${\bf\Gamma}_{2}(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})_{2}]_{n}$ of one-dimensional pore networks. They measured $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf D}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of the MOF at 40 and $70~\mathrm{K}$ and then compared them with GCMC simulated isotherms where the OPLS-AA was used. Their simulation showed a tendency to slightly overestimate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf D}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms although quantum effect was considered.
Some research groups studied imaginary MOFs (not synthesized yet) for improved design to increase $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity through GCMC simulations. Zhang et al.82 designed five new MOF materials by exchanging the organic linker (BDC) of MOF-5, where BDC was replaced with oxalate and with new organic linkers by introducing $-\mathrm{F}$ , $-\mathrm{Cl}$ , $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , and $-\mathrm{CCl}_{3}$ to tone up the electronegativity of linkers. Then they simulated their $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms up to 1 bar at $77~\mathrm{K}$ using the DREIDING69 FF and found that the proposed MOFs showed much higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake behavior at low pressure (up to 1 bar).82 Since the current MOFs show very low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount at room temperature, Frost and Snurr83 investigated how much the heat of adsorption should be increased to meet the current DOE target by artificially increasing the hydrogen–MOF Lennard-Jones attraction with GCMC simulations. They found that if MOF materials can achieve an isosteric heat of $10{-}15\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ with a free volume between 1.6 and $2.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $6\%$ could be achieved, and MOFs with free volumes less than $1.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ or void fractions of less than $75\%$ will need isosteric heats larger than $20\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ to achieve $6\%$ and 30 g cm3 of H2.83
GCMC simulation work on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in COF systems was first reported by Giovanni Garberoglio in 2007.84 He simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for 3-dimensional COFs (COF-102, COF-103, COF-105, and COF-108) at 77 and
$298\mathrm{~K~}$ using $\mathrm{UFF}^{63}$ and DREIDING,69 and showed that the best COF is COF-105 (maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $\sim10.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and COF-108 (maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of ${\sim}0.9\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ) at $298\mathrm{K}$ . Another similar work was reported by Klontzas et al.85 Using an empirical Lennard-Jones potential,86 they predicted that the gravimetric uptake for COF-108 reaches a value of $21\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 100 bar and $4.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ at room temperature and 100 bar.85 GCMC simulation work on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in 2-dimensional COFs (COF-6, COF-8, and $\mathrm{COF-10})^{19}$ was performed with the DREIDING69 FF by Garberoglio and Vallauri.87
Most GCMC works introduced above so far used empirical FFs such as UFF,63 DREIDING,69 and OPLS-AA,65 where the $\mathrm{UFF}^{63}$ and DREIDING69 were developed by our group. All of the three FFs were developed for predicting structures and dynamics of organic, biological, and main-group inorganic molecules, mainly for covalent bonds between atoms. Thus they would predict rough binding energies for non-bonded interactions between $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and MOFs, so that combination of the GCMC simulation and the FFs might provide inaccurate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs and COFs. In the previous GCMC studies the hydrogen molecule is treated as a diatomic molecule modeled by a Lennard-Jones potential developed from Buch88 or Darkrim and Levesque.89
However we developed accurate non-bonded FFs for $\mathrm{H}_{2^{--}}\mathrm{MOFs}^{48,90}$ (or COFs)91 and $\mathrm{H}_{2^{--}}\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\ 92}$ from high level ab initio calculations where all of our FFs describe interactions between atoms. From the ab initio based FFs, we showed accurate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for cubic48 and hexagonal90 crystalline IRMOFs, and 2D-91 and 3D-COFs.91 For example, our simulations48 for IRMOF-1 indicate $1.28\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, close to an experimental result14 of $1.30\mathrm{wt\%}$ under the same conditions, and show 4.17 and $4.89\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at pressures of 20 and 50 bar and 77K, which are also comparable to the experimental results16 of 4.5 and $4.9\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ under the same conditions. On the other hand, previous GCMC simulations with empirical FFs for IRMOF-1 at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar showed $1.62~\mathrm{wt\%}$ by Sagara et al., $,^{35}\ 1.33\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ by Yang and Zhong,64 $1.38~\mathrm{wt\%}$ by Garberoglio et al., $^{66}1.33\mathrm{wt\%}$ by Frost et al.,76 and $1.27\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ by Zhang et al.,58 which are similar to our simulation except for Sagara et al.35 However at $77~\mathrm{~K~}$ and pressures of 20 and 50 bar, Yang and Zhong64 showed $\sim6.5$ and ${\sim}8.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ , Garberoglio et al.66 showed $\sim6.3$ and $\sim7.0~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ , and Frost et al.76 showed $\sim7.8$ $w t\%$ and ${\sim}8.7~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ , respectively, which are overestimated in comparison to the experimental values.16 Such overestimation is more pronounced as pressures increase. Our simulation technique reproduces well $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for hexagonal $\mathbf{MOF-}177^{93}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ in which an experiment16 exhibits the maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $7.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 70 bar, very similar to our simulation result $(7.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar).90 We also simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of COFs and then compared them with an experiment.91 For COF-5, our simulation is in excellent agreement with an experiment (3.3 vs. $3.4\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at 50 bar) performed in the work.91 Recently we have also developed ab initio based FFs for calculation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake in COFs and found that our FFs do reproduce well experimental $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms.94 As shown so far, the ab initio based GCMC simulation can provide more accurate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms than simulations with empirical FFs. In addition, differently from previous GCMC works, we do not use the NIST database26 for $\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}$ in eqn (1) in calculating the excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount. The Gibbs surface excess is the absolute amount of gas contained in the pores minus the amount of gas $(\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}V_{\mathrm{pore}}$ in eqn (1)) that would be present in the pores in the absence of gas–solid intermolecular forces.95 In calculating the $\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}V_{\mathrm{pore}}$ , we turn off all the attractive interaction parts between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and MOFs (or COFs) in our developed FFs (while $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction terms are used as is) and then perform additional GCMC simulations at each temperature and pressure. This fact means that our simulation technique for the calculation of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount does not use any experimental information (except crystal information) to calculate the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount, thus it is a pure theoretical approach.
## 2.3 MD simulations on $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ diffusion in MOFs and COFs
For practical hydrogen storage media, the kinetic properties of hydrogen are also very important together with high hydrogen uptake capacity. To investigate the kinetic properties such as diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in MOFs or COFs, MD simulation is appropriate.
The first MD simulation on the diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules in MOFs was reported by Yang and Zhong64 where they calculated the self-diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in IRMOF-1, -8, and -18 as a function of pressure at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . The simulation indicated that the diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in IRMOF-18 is much lower than diffusion in the other two MOFs due to the steric hindrance effects of the pendant $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ groups in IRMOF-18, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules diffuse more rapidly in IRMOF-8 than in IRMOF-1 because of the relatively larger pore size of IRMOF-8. The second MD simulation on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion in IRMOF-1 was reported by Skoulidas and $\mathrm{\Sholl^{96}}$ where the simulation was performed at room temperature. They calculated the self-diffusivity and transport diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed in the MOF as a function of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ loading (pressure), and found that the self-diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in IRMOF-1 decreases as the loading is increased while the transport diffusivity increases monotonically.
Here, the self-diffusivity describes the diffusive motion of a single particle $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ and the transport diffusivity indicates the transport of mass and the decay of density fluctuations in the system.97 In general, the self-diffusivity is theoretically measured under equilibrium MD simulation, while the transport diffusivity is measured under non-equilibrium MD simulation conditions in which finite concentration gradients exist.97
In 2008, some research groups reported $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion in various MOFs using MD simulations. Liu et al.98 calculated the self-diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in ten different IRMOFs with and without interpenetration (catenation) and showed that catenation can reduce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusivity by a factor of 2 to 3 at room temperature as well as the bigger free volume leads to a larger $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusivity. Keskin et al.99 investigated the self-diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Cu-BTC}^{72}$ MOF. Liu et al.79 calculated the selfdiffusivity and transport diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{ted})_{0.5}]$ MOF synthesized in the work and found that the diffusivities of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the MOF are comparable to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in IRMOF-1 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . And Salles et al.100 calculated the self-diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in two MOFs, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}47(\mathrm{V})^{101}$ and $\mathrm{\mathbf{MIL-}}53(\mathrm{Cr}),$ 102 and showed that the hydrogen diffusivity at low loading is about 2 orders of magnitude higher than in zeolites, which was also first supported by the quasielastic neutron scattering experiment. They also applied the simulational and experimental methods to CH4 diffusion in the same MOFs.103
A MD simulation study on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion in COFs was first tried by Garberoglio and Vallauri.87 They calculated the selfand transport diffusivities of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in 2D-COFs (COF-6, COF-8 and COF-10)19 and showed that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion in the COFs is one order of magnitude more rapid than in MOFs. As far as we know, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusivity in 3D-COFs has not been reported yet.
All the MD simulation studies reviewed above are on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion inside MOFs or COFs. Studies on the interfacial region between the gas phase of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and MOFs (or COFs) have not been reported yet although it would also provide useful information to understand the real kinetics of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . This could be a good theoretical topic for future works. Moreover, all of the MD works used empirical FFs such as $\mathrm{UFF}^{63}$ and DREIDING69 during the MD simulation, thus we expect that the accuracy of the MD simulation could be improved by using our ab initio based FFs.48,90–92
## 2.4 FFs for prediction of crystal structures of MOFs and COFs
To date, most experimental and theoretical research groups have used the standard $\mathrm{UFF}^{63}$ and DREIDING69 to build or predict crystal structures of MOFs and COFs. The FFs have provided reasonable crystal information, comparing with experimental X-ray diffractions, and have reproduced well peculiar properties of MOFs such as negative thermal expansion.53,104–106
On the other hand, studies on FF development for providing accurate crystal structures of MOFs and COFs were recently performed by Tafipolsky et al.,107 Huang et al.,108 Greathouse and Allendorf,109 and Schmid and Tafipolsky.110 Tafipolsky et al.107 developed a new $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{M}3^{111}$ FF for IRMOF-1 from DFT and ab initio calculations. The FF predicts the IRMOF-1 structure successfully and yields vibrational frequencies in reasonable agreement with the predictions of DFT. It is also encouraging that the self-diffusivity of a benzene obtained from MD simulation with the FF is within $\sim30\%$ of the value measured by NMR.112 Huang et al.108 developed a FF from DFT calculations and experimental data and then calculated the phonon thermal conductivity and vibrational power spectra of IRMOF-1. Greathouse and Allendorf109 modified the original $\mathrm{CVFF^{113}}$ for IRMOF-1 with previous DFT and experimental results on the negative thermal expansion property, elastic moduli, and vibrational power spectra. And Schmid and Tafipolsky110 developed a FF for COF-102 and the FF was validated with DFT results on crystal information and vibrational modes of COF-102.
3. Strategies for improved designs of MOFs and COFs with high hydrogen storage capacity
In 2005, Rowsell and Yaghi114 systematically discussed six strategies (high porosity with appropriate pore size, impregnation, catenation, open metal sites, MOFs with light metals, and functionalized linkers) for high hydrogen adsorption in MOFs. The paper opened a new window for improved hydrogen storage in MOFs (and COFs), and since then many developments have been reported with help of the paper. Thus, in this review we will update and extend the strategies mentioned in the paper114 and additionally introduce some new strategies.
## 3.1 Appropriate pore size
As soon as the possibility of MOFs as a hydrogen storage medium was reported,13 many scientists have focused on the relationship between pore size (or surface area) of the MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage amount, and they have shown that the gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage amount is linearly proportional to the pore size (or surface area).15,16,48,73,76,90,115–123
Lin et al.117 synthesized three MOFs with NbO-type topologies using $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(O_{2}C R)_{4}]}$ paddle-wheel units and biphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid, terphenyl- $_{3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}.}$ - tetracarboxylic acid, and quaterphenyl- $3,3^{\prime\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic acid and then investigated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption behaviors as a function of their pore volume. They found the maximum amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed increased with increasing pore size, while the maximum adsorbate density decreased with increasing pore size, indicating that the contrasting adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ density with increasing pore size suggests that an optimum pore size exists. Therefore it was concluded that a strategy of only increasing pore volume may not give the optimum hydrogen storage material.
Also, we studied the effect of pore volume on the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage amount in cubic crystalline IRMOFs (Fig. 1) using GCMC simulation with ab initio based FFs.48 Fig. 2 shows total and excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake for the five MOFs (shown in Fig. 1) in gravimetric and volumetric units at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and $300\mathrm{~K~}$ .48 At $77\mathrm{K}$ , gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake (Fig. 2(a)) increases with increasing organic linker size (pore size). For example, at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 100 bar MOF-C6 (IRMOF-1) has an excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $5.09\mathrm{\wt\%}$ (total uptake: $6.46~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ), MOF-C10 (IRMOF-8) has an excess uptake of $5.69~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (total uptake: $7.52~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ),
MOF-C16 (IRMOF-14) has an excess uptake of $6.43\mathrm{\wt\%}$ (total uptake: $9.32~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ), MOF-C22 has an excess uptake of $6.84~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ (total uptake: $11.02~\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ ), and MOF-C30 has an excess uptake of $6.98~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (total uptake: $12.13~\mathrm{{\wt^{0}}\%}$ . However volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake (Fig. 2(b)) generally decreases with increasing pore size, so that the best MOF in the volumetric unit is MOF-C6 with the smallest pore size. As shown here, gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake is linearly proportional to the pore size of the MOFs, while volumetric uptake is generally inversely proportional to the pore size. For practical hydrogen storage materials, volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity is a very important factor together with gravimetric uptake. Thus similar to Lin et al.,117 increasing the pore volume is not the only factor to be considered in the design of hydrogen storage materials. And the conclusion is also supported by Fig. 2(c) where the maximum gravimetric excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at $300~\mathrm{K}$ is near $1.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 100 bar although total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake is up to
$2.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at this pressure. This result indicates that one might not be able to approach the DOE target by only increasing the pore size.
Similarly we also investigated the relationship between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake and pore volume of MOFs with hexagonal structures using GCMC simulation, and obtained a similar conclusion.90
Some research groups have studied the optimum pore size for graphite using ab initio or DFT calculations, and all of them reached the same conclusion that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy is maximized up to $-13.0\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ with an interlayer distance of 6 A˚ .124–127 (Here the $6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ is just an interlayer distance of graphite, meaning a value obtained by ignoring van der Waals radii of carbon atoms in graphite. When considering the van der Waals radius, the pore size is close to the kinetic diameter $(2.89\mathrm{~\AA~})$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ since the maximal attraction of an adsorbate would occur at a size the same as the diameter of the adsorbate.) The magic number $(6\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ is applicable to MOFs or COFs because their organic linkers consist of aromatic carbon rings (e.g. benzene) and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction with the carbon rings is similar with graphene.
Of current MOFs and COFs, COF-1 representatively has a pore size near the magic number. Since COF-1 has a graphitelike structure with an $\cdot_{A B A B},$ stacking layer sequence,18 its pore size is two times that of one layer distance, leading to $\overset{\cdot}{6.7}\mathring\mathrm{\textbf{A}}$ $(2\times3.35\mathrm{~\AA~})$ , as shown in Fig. 3. According to our previous GCMC simulation with ab initio based FFs, COF-1 has a very high heat of adsorption for $\mathrm{H}_{2}(8.8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ due to the appropriate pore size, leading to exceptional $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity at low pressure $(1.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 0.1 bar) which is much higher than other COFs (COF-5, COF-102, COF-103, COF-105, and COF-108). Also, COF-1 shows the highest volumetric uptake up to 20 bar among the COFs considered in the work and a peculiar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption behavior so that the ratio of excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake to total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at 100 bar is 0.99.
However, at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ , COF-1 still shows a low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage amount (total $0.78\mathrm{\wt\%}$ and excess $0.75\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at 100 bar). To achieve a high adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at room temperature and moderate pressures, the heat of adsorption should be in the range of $20{-}30\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ,114 higher than that $(8.8\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ) in COF-1.
## 3.2 Impregnation
To make effective pore sizes in MOFs for high adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , Yaghi and co-workers114 suggested the insertion of another adsorbate surface within large-pore MOFs. For example, they experimentally proved that large molecules such as $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ and Reichardt’s dye can be included into MOF-177 from the solution phase,93 and suggested that the impregnation with such inclusion could provide the more attractive sites that are ultimately necessary to improve $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake.114 Nevertheless, as far as we know, no studies on the impregnation have been reported yet. Thus we investigated the effect of the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ inclusion in MOF-177 on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake using GCMC simulation.
Fig. 4 shows the $\mathrm{C}_{60}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}177$ structures obtained from our GCMC simulation at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar with the standard DREIDING69 FF, in which MOF-177 can absorb sixteen $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ molecules in the unit cell under these conditions. And the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ inclusion leads to a decrease in the free volume of the MOF to $0.61~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ from $1.54~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for pure MOF-177.
From the $\mathrm{C}_{60}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}177$ structure of Fig. 4, we simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ and $300~\mathrm{K}$ with our ab initio based FFs,48,90–92 and then compared them with those of MOF-177, shown in Fig. 5. The inclusion of $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ into MOF-177 increases $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at low pressure compared with pure MOF-177 at 77 and $300~\mathrm{K}$ . However due to the lower pore size of $\mathrm{C}_{60}@\mathrm{MOF}–177$ , the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity at high pressure is lower than MOF-177. We desired that the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ inclusion would be positioned near the center of the pores in MOF-177 to minimize dead volumes for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage. However since the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ attractively interacts with MOF-177 and another $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ , it could block the existing adsorptive sites on the MOF-177 (although it also provides additional adsorptive sites on $\mathrm{C}_{60})$ . At $300\mathrm{~K~}$ , the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ inclusion slightly improves the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of MOF-177, nevertheless the uptake amount is still much lower than the DOE target of $6.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , indicating that impregnation might not be an effective alternative for practical hydrogen storage. In addition, it is noticeable that $\mathrm{C}_{60}@\mathrm{MOF}–177$ shows a smaller gap between total and excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake than MOF-177.
## 3.3 Catenation
Framework catenation is another way to tune the pore size of MOFs.129 Catenation is divided into two types: interpenetration130 and interweaving (Fig. 6).131 Interpenetration is that the frameworks are maximally displaced from each other by shifting the second framework exactly one half of the pore size in the $x,y$ , and $z$ directions; the interwoven MOFs minimize the distance between both frameworks without atomic overlap.114
Kesanli et al.132 experimentally showed a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption amount $1.12~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 48 bar) at room temperature with interpenetrated MOFs, Sun et al.133 synthesized interwoven MOFs showing a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $1.90~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, and Rowsell and $\mathrm{Yaghi}^{134}$ measured $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities of various MOFs and then found that catenated MOFs show the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at low pressure below 1 bar. After the results, some experimental studies135–138 on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in catenated MOFs were reported.
The theoretical study on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in interpenetrated MOFs (IRMOF-9, -11, and -13) was first reported by Jung et al.77 using GCMC simulations at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ . The simulation shows that the small pores generated by the catenation play a role in confining the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules more densely, indicating that the interpenetrated MOFs have higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake than the non-interpenetrated MOFs at low pressure. However due to the small pores caused by the catenation the interpenetrated MOFs have lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at high pressure. They also clarified the binding sites of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the interpenetrated MOFs where the adsorption sites with the largest binding energies are located in the very small volume surrounded by two zinc oxo clusters (metal–metal sites), the adsorption sites with the second largest binding energies are the metal–linker sites confined by a zinc oxo cluster of one chain and an organic linker of the other framework, and the adsorption sites with the lowest binding energies are the linker–linker sites, occupying the largest portion in the interpenetrated MOFs.
Ryan et al.78 investigated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in both interpenetrated and interwoven MOFs (IRMOF-1, -10, and -16) at 77 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ by GCMC simulations. Similar to Jung et al.,77 they showed that at low pressures catenation is clearly beneficial for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake, but at high pressures non-catenated MOFs exhibit higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake than their catenated MOFs. Their simulation for $298\mathrm{K}$ showed that in a gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake unit the loading for the catenated structures is approximately one half that of the non-catenated structures, while in a volumetric unit a similar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount is shown for catenated and non-catenated MOFs.
From the two simulational works,77,78 we can conclude that catenation is helpful for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and low pressures, but it could not be at room temperature.
## 3.4 Open metal sites in the metal oxide parts of MOFs
So far, we have discussed improvement in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs through control of pore size. However the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity is also improved using open metal sites in MOFs leading to stronger $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding.
According to G. J. Kubas,139 the d orbitals of a transition metal (M) interact with antibonding orbitals of a hydrogen molecule, leading to energetic stabilization of the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ bond. The transition metal–hydrogen complexes $(\mathrm{M}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2})$ can reversibly bind $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and the binding energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to the transition metal can be varied between 20–160 kJ mol1,139 which is around the appropriate heat of adsorption range $(20{-}30\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})^{114}$ for high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at room temperature. Currently, the Kubas binding has been applied to enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding with open metal (transition metal) sites in MOFs.
There are many experimental studies reporting high binding energies using the open metal sites of MOFs.17,74,140–153 Forster et al.140,143 synthesized MOFs with exposed $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites and the MOFs have high enthalpies of adsorption $(9.4-10.4\mathrm{~\kJ~{~}~m o l^{-1}})$ . Yaghi and co-workers reported MOF-505 with open sites of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ where the MOF shows a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amount of $2.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.74 Long and co-workers17 synthesized a MOF with exposed $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ sites and the MOF has a maximum heat of adsorption of $10.1\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . Long and co-worker also experimentally exchanged the guest $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ion in $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{BTT})_{8}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}$ $(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{T}\ =\ 1,3,5.$ -benzenetristetrazolate) MOF with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ where the metals are unsaturated, and then investigated their $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake behaviors.146 The new MOFs exhibited high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities ranging from 2.00 to $2.29~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 900 torr, and the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ -exchanged MOF showed an initial enthalpy of adsorption of $10.5~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ .146 Zhou and co-worker142 synthesized a new MOF (PCN-9) with $\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{O}\l $ (carboxylate)4 secondary building units similar to the active center of hemoglobin (open) and the MOF has a heat of adsorption of $10.1\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ showing a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $1.53\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. Similarly they also recently reported PCN-12 with open $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ sites where the MOF shows the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake $(3.05~\mathrm{wt\%})$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar.148 And, Vitillo et al.150 reported a MOF (called CPO-27-Ni) with an initial heat of adsorption of $-13.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , the highest yet observed for a MOF where the CPO-27-Ni has open $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites and a similar crystal structure to the typical MOF-74.154 Recently Dinca˜ and $\mathrm{Long}^{153}$ reviewed in detail experimental $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs with exposed metal sites. The exposed metal sites must increase the heat of adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and then lead to an increase in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amounts at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ and low pressures (e.g. 1 bar), however there are no reports on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake behavior at room temperature. Since the heat of adsorption is lower than the appropriate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy $(20{-}30~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}})^{114}$ for high uptake around room temperature, we think that the current MOFs mentioned above might still have low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake amounts at room temperature although they would show higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at room temperature than other MOFs without open metal sites.
A theoretical study on these open metal sites of MOFs was first reported by Yang and Zhong.75 They investigated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption sites in $\mathrm{MOF}{-}505^{74}$ by GCMC simulation and DFT calculations where they reported a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy of $-13.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ to the open metal site in the MOF. Also they predicted that the MOF has a low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity at room temperature (e.g. $0.82~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 50 bar). Also, Kosa et al.155 calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies to exposed $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ sites using DFT calculations where the exposed $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ coordinated sites were modelled by neutral square pyramidal clusters, $\mathbf{ML}_{3}\mathbf{L^{\prime}}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{\small~L~}=\mathrm{\CH}_{3}\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ and ${\bf{L}}^{\prime}\mathrm{~=~}$ $\mathrm{OCH}_{3}^{-}$ . In the work, they showed that the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ site has stronger $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies (6 to $23\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ) than the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ site $(\sim1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ).
Sun et al.156 clarified the characteristics of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to exposed $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ sites in a MOF synthesized by Long and co-workers17 through DFT calculations. As shown in Fig. 7, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to the exposed metal site results from the coupling between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\ \upsigma$ and $\mathbf{M}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}\ \ensuremath{\mathrm{d}_{z^{2}}}$ orbitals. They also replaced the Mn element in the original MOFs with early transition metals (Sc, Ti, V, and $\mathrm{Cr}$ ) and calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies are $21.9~\mathrm{{\kJ}~\ m o l^{-1}}$ to Sc-MOF, $34.6~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ to Ti-MOF, $46.5~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ to V-MOF, $10.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ to $\mathrm{Cr-MOF}$ , and $8.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ to Mn-MOF (the experimental binding energy for $\mathbf{Mn-MOF}$ is $10.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\cdot$ ),17 indicating that the binding energy to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can be tuned from about 10 to $50\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ by using different transition metals in MOF systems.
3.5 Open metal sites in the organic linker parts of MOFs (or COFs)
The trial was recently performed by Kaye and Long.157 They synthesized the MOF-5 with $\mathrm{Cr}$ metal centers attached to the benzene rings in an ${\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{\eta}}}^{6}$ fashion through the chemical reactions of MOF-5, $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ , dibutyl ether, and THF. Also they measured the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption amount in the MOF-5 with the $\mathrm{Cr}$ metal centers at $298\mathrm{K}$ , however the adsorption amount is fewer than 0.2 molecules per formula unit.
After the experimental findings,157 Lochan et al.158 investigated interactions between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and the model of exposed $\mathrm{Cr}$ metal sites (half-sandwich piano-stool shape complex) by DFT calculations. Fig. 8 shows optimized structures of $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6})\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{H}_{2})_{n}$ complexes from DFT calculations. Here, one $\mathrm{Cr}$ atom can bind up to three $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules (4, 5, and 6 complexes in Fig. 8). In the $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6})\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{H}_{2})_{3}$ complex (4), one of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules (H3–H4) parallel to the benzene ring has a bond distance of $0.882\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ which is shorter than in the remaining two $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules $(0.895\mathrm{~\AA~})$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dissociation energies for 4, 5, and 6 complexes are 63.0, 65.9, and $76.3\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively, which are stronger than the ideal binding energy range $(20-30\mathrm{~kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ). Thus these complexes may suffer from desorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at ambient conditions, however the higher binding energies indicate that it should be possible to achieve higher sorption capacities at room temperature, which is contrary to the experiment of Kaye and Long.157 Lochan et al.158 also considered other metals $(\mathrm{V}^{-},\mathrm{Mn}^{+}$ , Mo, and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ ) instead of $\mathrm{Cr}$ and found that Mo and $\mathrm{{Mn}^{+}}$ increase the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy by $84.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ , while $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ leads to a decrease in the binding energy by $41.5\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , and $\mathrm{\DeltaV^{-}}$ shows a similar binding energy to the $\mathrm{Cr}$ case. In addition, similar to the MOF-5 with $\mathrm{Cr}$ metal centers,157 Ti decorated MOF-5 was also proposed from DFT calculations.159
Incorporating coordinatively unsaturated metal centers within the organic linkers would be an alternative to enhance hydrogen storage capacity of MOFs at room temperature. However, the metal atoms bound to the organic linkers (e.g. benzene rings) in an ${\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{\eta}}}^{6}$ fashion may have a lower binding energy to the organic linker than the cohesive energy of the metal crystal, leading to aggregation (clustering) of the metal,160 which is also suggested by Kaye and Long.157 If such clustering takes place, the Kubas interaction139 between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and transition atoms becomes invalid. Owing to this clustering of the metal element, experimental $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOF-5 with $\mathrm{Cr}$ metal centers on the benzene rings is very low at room temperature.157 The clustering of transition metals in MOFs was also experimentally proven.161,162
This clustering of exposed metal atoms could be prevented by using another organic linker such as $\mathrm{\Delta[(Bipydc)M(CO)_{4}]^{2-}}$ (Bipydc $=2\AA,2\AA^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- ${5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) which is shown in Fig. 9(a). We calculated the binding energies of $\mathbf{M}^{n+}$ ( $\mathbf{M}=$ Sc, Ti, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu and $Z\mathrm{n}$ , and $n=0$ , 1 and 2) to the Bipydc ligand by DFT calculations and then compared them with the cohesive energies of the transition metals. For $n=0$ , only Mn element has a stronger binding energy to the
Bipydc ligand than its cohesive energy. For $n=1$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}^{+}$ have stronger binding energies to the ligand. And in the case of $\textit{n}=\textit{2}$ , all metal elements are favorable for formation of the $\mathrm{(Bipydc)}\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ complexes. Moreover, we investigated interactions between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and the $(\mathrm{Bipydc})\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ complexes and found that in (Bipydc) $\mathbf{\{M}}^{2+}(\mathbf{H}_{2})_{4}$ the average $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies per $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule are from $-24.6\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to $-62.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{V}^{2+}$ , where the optimized structure of (Bipydc) $\mathsf{N}^{2+}(\mathrm{H}_{2})_{4}$ is shown in Fig. 9(b). Since most of the binding energies are in the ideal values range for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage at room temperature, MOFs or COFs with the $({\mathrm{Bipydc}}){\mathrm{M}}^{2+}$ complexes would be promising for practical hydrogen storage. We will report the detailed results in a future paper.
3.6 Doping of alkali elements onto the organic linker parts of MOFs (or COFs)
In 2007, we first proposed Li-doped MOFs as a practical hydrogen storage material using an ab initio based GCMC simulation shown in Fig. 10.163 And predicted gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for the Li-doped MOFs at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ are shown in Fig. 11. At $300~\mathrm{K}$ , pure MOFs without Li doping lead to a low excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $<1\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ even at 100 bar although the total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake is $2.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $300\mathrm{K}$ and 100 bar, which is too low for practical use. However for Li-doped MOFs we predict significantly improved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at room temperature. For example, at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 20 bar we calculate that Li-MOF-C30 binds excess $3.89\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (total $4.21\mathrm{\wt{\%}H_{2})}$ , which goes up to $4.56~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (total $5.30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ). Besides, at a pressure of 100 bar, Li-MOF-C30 shows an excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $5.16~\mathrm{{\wt\%}}$ $(300~\mathrm{~K~})$ , $5.57\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $(273\mathrm{~K})$ , and 5.99 $w t\%$ (243 K), and a total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $6.47~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $(300~\mathrm{~K~})$ , $7.03\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $(273~\mathrm{K})$ , and $7.57~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (243 K), which reaches the
$2010~\mathrm{DOE}$ target of $6.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . For Li-doped MOFs the high electron affinity of the aromatic $\displaystyle\operatorname{sp}^{2}$ carbon framework promotes separation of the charge, making the Li positive (acidic), providing strong stabilization of molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ where the effective binding energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is $16.8~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ .
After publishing the paper on Li-doped MOFs, some similar theoretical works were reported.164–168 Blomqvist et al.164 showed using DFT calculations that two Li atoms are strongly adsorbed on six-carbon rings of the organic linker in $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}}$ -based MOF-5, one on each site, carrying a charge of $+0.9$ e per Li, and each Li can bind three $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules around itself with a binding energy of $12~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ . Mavrandonakis et al.165 showed that the Li atom is preferably located on the organic linker in MOFs rather than the metal oxide part, and Li is positively charged by almost $+1$ e. Upon interacting with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules, strong polarization effects are observed and a charge distribution of approximately $+0.1\mathrm{{e}}$ is transferred from the adsorbed three $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules to the Li atom, leading to very strong dipoles which is the reason for the strong binding.165 However, another theoretical work166 on $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}$ -based MOFs reported the result that the Li associates strongly with the metal oxide part and less so with the aromatic rings. Klontzas et al.167 calculated the hydrogen storage amount in MOFs modified by lithium alkoxide groups, and the Li-modified MOFs have a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 100 bar and $4.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at room temperature. Choi et al.168 considered Li-doped COFs and showed using DFT calculations that Li doping plays a beneficial role to enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of COFs.
The Li doping effect on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs was also experimentally reported by Mulfort and Hupp.169 They directly reduced a MOF of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{diPyNI})$ (ndc $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 2{,}6{-}$ naphthalenedicarboxylate, $\begin{array}{r c l}{{\mathrm{diPyNI}}}&{{=}}&{{N,N^{\prime}.}}\end{array}$ -di-(4-pyridyl)- 1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide) through a suspension of Li metal in DMF. This procedure allowed doping of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ cations into the MOF, and the Li-doped MOF led to an increase in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption from 0.93 to $1.63\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm. They also experimentally investigated effects of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ doping on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs.170 The experiment showed that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding is strongest with Li and decreases as $\mathrm{Li}^{+}>$ $\mathrm{\DeltaNa}^{+}~>~\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , however the uptake increases in the opposite order. The alkali-doped MOFs have the interwoven structure, which means that the alkali cations may be positioned between frameworks and thus not readily accessible to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The heat of adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the alkali-doped MOFs is smaller than calculated in the theoretical results163–165 mentioned above.
Next, we discuss the experimental synthesis of the Li-doped MOFs or COFs, especially aromatic lithium complexes in an ${\mathfrak{\eta}}^{6}$ coordination, from the literature since the Li-doped MOFs or COFs are most promising for practical hydrogen storage. Thus this examination of experimental evidence of the aromatic lithium complexes should provide important information for future experimental and theoretical studies.
The $\pi$ -complexes between lithium and benzene derivatives bonded through $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{\mathfrak{n}}}^{6}.$ -coordination has been reported in many times in the literature. Stucky and co-workers171 synthesized compound 7 (Fig. 12) which was synthesized under mild conditions from naphthalene and $n$ -butyllithium ( $\overset{\cdot}{n}$ -BuLi) in an ether–hexane solution. The procedure was undertaken at room temperature and in a glove box with an Ar atmosphere, which indicates the thermodynamic stability of the complex. However the compound is very sensitive to water and air.
In an analogous synthesis, Stucky and co-workers172 reported derivative 8 (Fig. 13), which was synthesized from 9,10-dihydroanthracene and $n$ -butyllithium. The solvent used was dry benzene and $N,N,N^{\prime},N^{\prime}$ -tetramethylethylenediamine under a nitrogen atmosphere. The lithium was added as $n$ -butyllithium in hexane. Finally the solution was kept under a He atmosphere and no heat was necessary. They noticed that when the mixing was done, the solution turned to dark purple which suggested the formation of the anthracene dianion. The crystal structure shows however that the benzene rings do not retain a planar orientation, as expected from the anthracenide fragment, which is antiaromatic. In a tour de force to form these complexes, Manceron and Andrews173 used cryogenic conditions $(\sim15\:\mathrm{K})$ to mix benzene and lithium metal in solid argon. The authors reach the conclusion that ${\mathrm{LiC}}_{6}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Li}(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6})_{2}$ was formed using IR and isotopic studies.
The chemistry of $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{\upeta}}^{6}.$ -benzene–Li complexes has proved to be feasible, however the $\upeta^{5}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cp}$ –Li system offers more versatility and stability. So if a porous material was to be targeted to form complexes with Li, $\mathrm{Cp}$ incorporation into the ligand would help to absorb the metal. Thus we propose some ligands with these characteristics in Fig. 14. Compounds analogous to 9 have been experimentally synthesized,174 and some have been coordinated to transition metals.175 Compound 10 is proposed to form a compound analogous to MOF-177. The source of Li for most of these systems comes from $n$ -BuLi and the common solvents used are toluene, hexane, THF, and TMEDA. Common solvents used in the formation of microporous materials include these solvents, so there is an opportunity window to generate impregnation in solution with Li. Moreover the temperatures at which the reactions were executed include room temperature so the kinetic parameter should not be an obstacle. Lithium metal has been tried as the Li source but the reaction conditions are harsh so it is not a recommended path to obtain Li.
## 3.7 Substitution of the metal oxide parts in MOFs
Substitution of metal oxide units in MOFs with a lighter metal element could improve the hydrogen storage amount in gravimetric units owing to the lighter weight of the MOFs.114 Fe´ rey et al.176 synthesized $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-C}_{6}\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{-CO}_{2})$ $\textbf{M}=$ ${\bf A l}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}.$ ) called MIL-53 and found that ${\bf A l}^{3+}$ leads to a higher hydrogen storage amount of $3.8~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ than ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ $(3.1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and $1.6~\mathrm{MPa}$ , showing that the lighter element is beneficial in gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake. Dinca˜ and Long177 synthesized a MOF using $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ ions and the MOF showed $0.46~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 880 torr. And Farha et al.178 synthesized carborane (icosahedral carbon-containing boron clusters) based MOFs and the MOF stored $2.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm.
The substitution of metal oxides in MOFs can lead to an increase in the heat of adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . In Prussian blue analogues M3[Co(CN)6]2 (M = Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn),179 the heat of adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is changed with the metal and the order is $\mathrm{\DeltaNi>Cu>Co>Fe>Zn>Mn}$ , with the heat of adsorption in the range of 5.3 to $7.0\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , much lower than the ideal value $(20-30\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ ) for practical hydrogen storage. Another similar work was also recently published.180
We also clarified the effects of the substitution of transition metals with lighter elements $\mathbf{Mg}$ and Be) on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in the MOFs using ab initio and GCMC methodologies.48 In the work, we used the MOF structures shown in Fig. 1. From MP2 calculations, we find that the $\mathrm{Mg_{4}O(C O_{2})_{6}H_{6}}$ cluster shows a slightly stronger $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy $(-6.78\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ) than that $(-6.24~\mathrm{~kJ~}\mathrm{~mol}^{-1})$ of original $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}(C O_{2})_{6}H_{6}}}$ , while $\mathrm{{Be_{4}O(C O_{2})_{6}H_{6}}}$ has a lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy $(-4.40\ \mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1})}$ . Simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities of the $\mathrm{Mg\mathrm{-}}$ and Be-MOFs are shown in Fig. 15. The substitution of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ and Be increases $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in the MOFs. For $77\mathrm{K}$ , excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at 1 bar is $2.68~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for $\mathbf{Mg-MOF}$ , $1.38~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for Be-MOF, and $1.28~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for $Z\mathrm{n-MOF}$ , and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at 100 bar is $7.63\mathrm{\wt\%}$ for $\mathbf{Mg-MOF}$ , $8.26~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for Be-MOF, and $5.09\mathrm{\wt\%}$ for $Z\mathrm{n-MOF}$ . Here, due to the strongest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy to the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ cluster, the $\mathbf{Mg-MOF}$ has the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at low pressure (1 bar). It is noticeable that although the Be cluster has the weakest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity is better than the Zn-MOF due to it having the lightest weight. However, at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ both the $\mathrm{Mg\mathrm{-}}$ and Be-MOFs show much lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage amounts than the $2010\ \mathrm{\DOE}$ target $(6.0~\mathrm{\wt\%})$ although they show higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage than the $Z_{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ , indicating that this substitution of the metal oxide part with lighter elements may not guarantee the practical use of MOFs as a hydrogen storage material although this way would be helpful at cryogenic temperatures.
## 3.8 Functionalized organic linkers
As a way to increase the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ physisorption energy of MOFs, the functionalization of organic linkers would show a good effect. Currently, organic linkers of most MOFs have aromatic backbones such as benzene and naphthalene. According to the ab initio calculations performed so far, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ physisorption energy to aromatic organic linkers increases with the addition of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ , and $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups due to their ability to enrich the aromatic system electronically, however the energy increase is not significant.30 The binding energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to benzene is $3.91\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , and it is $4.52~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $4.40~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , and $4.00~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ to $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}O H}$ .30 So the functionalized organic linkers could improve $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at low temperature, but not significantly at ambient temperature. And the functionalized organic linkers may decrease the pore size of the MOF, leading to decrease in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at high pressure.
We theoretically investigated the effects of a single-linked aromatic ring and a polyaromatic ring (Fig. 16) on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities of MOFs.90 In pure MOF cases, the polyaromatic ring shows higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at low pressure due to the higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ heat of adsorption. Our MP2 calculations revealed that more aromatic rings lead to higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies.48 For example, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies to benzene and naphthalene are 3.81 and $4.27~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ , respectively.48 However, a singlelinked aromatic ring has generally a higher surface area and free volume than a polyaromatic linker because exposing the latent edges of the six-membered rings leads to a significant enhancement in specific surface area. Thus at high pressures a single-linked aromatic ring can store more $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ than a polyaromatic ring. However, both linkers still show low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage amounts $(\sim1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ at room temperature. On the other hand, in the Li-doped MOFs, a single-linked aromatic ring occupies the only $\mathrm{C_{6}L i}$ composition irrespective of the number of carbon atoms, while in a polyaromatic ring the greater number of carbon atoms can show a higher lithium concentration, leading to a significant increase in the heat of adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ due to the strong interaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and Li.
## 3.9 Hydrogen spillover
The phenomenon of hydrogen spillover is defined as the dissociative chemisorption of hydrogen on the metal and the subsequent migration of atomic hydrogen onto the surface of the support such as alumina, carbon, and so on.181
For the first time, Li and ${\mathrm{Yang}}^{182}$ applied this hydrogen spillover technique to MOF materials to enhance hydrogen storage at room temperature. They investigated the hydrogen spillover effect on hydrogen storage in IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-8 by using $\mathbf{Pt}$ on active carbon $(\mathrm{Pt/AC})$ catalysts and then found that through this spillover of hydrogen atoms the hydrogen storage capacities of IRMOF-1 were significantly increased up to $1.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ from $0.4\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ for unmodified IRMOF-1 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $10\ \mathrm{MPa}$ .182 Similarly, the hydrogen storage amount of IRMOF-8 increased up to $1.8\mathrm{\wt{\%}}$ from $0.5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and $10\ \mathrm{MPa}$ through the hydrogen spillover technique.182
They also improved the spillover efficiency using carbon bridges at interfaces between the MOF and $\mathrm{Pt/AC}$ , and MOFs.183 These carbon bridges led to a significant increase in the hydrogen storage capacity of the MOFs at room temperature. At $298~\mathrm{~K~}$ and $10~\mathrm{\MPa}$ , IRMOF-1 stored $3\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and IRMOF-8 stored $4\mathrm{\mt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Moreover, this technique was also applied to MOF-177.184 Here it is also noticeable that the hydrogen storage capacity by the hydrogen spillover is reversible.
Although hydrogen storage by spillover must be a promising technique to achieve significant hydrogen storage in MOFs, the topic has been not actively studied theoretically. The only theoretical work was reported by Li et al.185 where they calculated adsorption energies of a hydrogen atom in MOFs with DFT calculations.
Our research group recently developed a new simulational paradigm called a reactive force field (ReaxFF).186 With this ReaxFF, one can accurately simulate chemical reactions (bond formation and breaking) in large systems. Thus, we expect that this simulation technique will be very helpful to study the hydrogen spillover phenomenon in MOFs.
## 4. Conclusions
This critical review summarizes the state of the art for theoretical studies of hydrogen storage in MOFs and COFs, showing that in silico methodology can provide important information (e.g. hydrogen adsorption site, hydrogen storage capacity, and hydrogen diffusion) to guide development of improved hydrogen storage materials. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption sites in MOFs and COFs are based on quantum mechanical calculations, either ab initio (e.g. MP2) or DFT. Hydrogen storage capacity in these porous materials can be predicted using GCMC simulations. Here, the accuracy of the GCMC simulation depends on the accuracy of the FFs describing the interatomic interactions for host–guest and guest–guest. Many, theoretical studies have used empirical FFs for the GCMC simulation, which have led to discrepancies between simulation and experiment. This problem can be solved by using FFs developed from accurate ab initio calculations. Here, we summarize our previous results showing that ab initio based GCMC simulations reproduce well experimental excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for MOFs and COFs leading to accurate total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ predictions. Moreover, kinetic properties of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in MOFs and COFs can be predicted by MD simulations.
Based on theoretical and experimental studies on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in MOFs and COFs, we discussed nine strategies to improve the hydrogen storage capacity of MOFs and COFs. Among these strategies, only three (open transition metal sites within the organic linker using the Kubas interaction, doping of alkali elements on the organic linker, and hydrogen spillover) lead to high hydrogen storage at room temperature. For example, with Li-doped MOFs we predict $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage of more than $6\%$ near room temperature, meeting the $2010~\mathrm{DOE}$ target. However, the other six strategies would be helpful at cryogenic temperatures. We hope that this review will provide useful guidelines for improved designs of MOFs or COFs as a hydrogen storage material.
